ture":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"close_class":"flaky","comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Initial rebar-debug Phase 1 evidence (investigation intentionally stopped for separate ownership): exact extras-enforced full suite failed only this node after 1:33:35 (13,582 passed); focused outer reproduction failed in 105.55s with `TimeoutExpired` after captured inner stdout already contained `.[100%]`. A direct inner pytest invocation under the same env printed `[100%]` and exited before a process snapshot could attach. A subsequent unchanged outer invocation passed in 1.49s. Therefore the defect is intermittent shutdown/liveness after successful test execution, not a deterministic assertion failure. Competing mechanisms remain unproven: non-daemon thread, live child, inherited capture pipe, or plugin teardown. No code or tests were edited, no retry was treated as resolution, and no RCA is claimed. Next owner must continue Phase 1 in a fresh origin/main worktree, quantify the failure rate, and capture the live process/thread/pipe state during an actual timeout before Phase 2.","timestamp":1786882943754037001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Phase 1 RCA — confirmed root cause\n\nThe nested pytest invocation in tests/_store_isolation.py:35-42 omits --basetemp even though the outer helper owns a tmp_path. The child therefore joins pytest's user-global numbered temp root. At pytest session finish, TempPathFactory closes its cleanup stack (_pytest/tmpdir.py:213-225, 315-340), which synchronously scans and recursively deletes stale numbered trees (_pytest/pathlib.py:279-290, 335-369). That unrelated global cleanup can outlive this helper's fixed 90-second communicate timeout after the selected test has already reported [100%]. A killed cleanup can leave garbage-* for the next run to resume, explaining the full-suite timeout, the immediately repeated focused timeout, and the later unchanged 1.49-second pass. The changeable defect is the missing private --basetemp argument at tests/_store_isolation.py:35-42.\n\nDiscriminating proof:\n- Controlled real-filesystem A/B with 400,000 stale entries: shared-root child 10.800s; private --basetemp child 0.882s, with the poisoned shared root untouched. A 20,000-entry run showed the same direction (1.030s vs 0.551s), establishing cleanup cost scales with retained entries.\n- SIGUSR1 at the exact post-[100%] boundary captured the main thread in shutil._rmtree_safe_fd -> _pytest.pathlib.rm_rf:167 -> maybe_delete_a_numbered_dir:290 -> cleanup_numbered_dir:367 -> _pytest.tmpdir.pytest_sessionfinish:340; the process then exited normally after 13.107s. This observes the predicted mechanism executing.\n- Exact ambient-store semantics stressed 25 times with a unique child basetemp while a 200,000-entry shared root remained poisoned: 25/25 passed, min 0.579s, median 0.607s, max 0.972s, poison_unchanged=True.\n- Rival hypothesis: an unbounded repo-isolation git status can be fault-injected to reproduce the same output boundary, but 100 normal measurements were median 0.0143s, p95 0.0164s, max 0.0610s and no natural stall stack identified Git. The observed cleanup stack plus private-basetemp A/B rules it out as this incident's cause.\n\nDefect legitimacy: defect. The existing contract at tests/unit/test_test_suite_store_isolation.py and tests/_store_isolation.py:32-43 is that the selected node completes within the bounded child run while ambient tracker HEAD/directories remain unchanged. Letting unrelated user-global pytest retention work consume that budget violates the helper's isolation invariant; no intentional dependency on shared temp cleanup exists.","timestamp":1786891102587139001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Phase 2 repair evidence. RED: the final held-out real-subprocess regression created four pre-existing numbered temp trees and a sentinel in another run's tree; before the fix, nested pytest deleted the sentinel and failed with 'nested pytest deleted another run's temp tree'. The fixer never saw this oracle. Fix: tests/_store_isolation.py now passes a caller-owned tmp_path/nested-pytest via --basetemp, isolating pytest session-finish cleanup without changing node selection, environment, timeout, capture, or ambient-store assertions. GREEN: the final two-test file passed; the exact omitted-basetemp mutation returned the sentinel test to RED; restoring the fix returned it to GREEN in 1.37s. Replication: 25/25 real nested-pytest outer-file runs passed (50 tests total), each about 2.2-2.7s, with no post-summary timeout. Safety: caller sweep found only the current unit and held-out tests; no behavioral dependency on shared temp cleanup, no production code or dependency change, and no existing assertion was weakened. Refactoring litmus passes because the oracle asserts observable filesystem survival, not private implementation structure. Sibling sweep: the independent nested collector tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py::_collect_node_ids has the same omitted-basetemp construct and is outside this helper path; filed and linked acidic-ferreous-mole with exact file impact and RED/GREEN plan. CLI/MCP/library/automated/dual-direction parity surfaces are N/A because this is a test-harness subprocess helper. Gates: focused held-out GREEN; mutation RED then restored GREEN; 25-run replication GREEN; make lint GREEN; make typecheck GREEN; make test GREEN with 13,584 passed, 17 skipped, 288 deselected, 20 warnings in 2,958.87s. Residual risk disposition: sibling filed as acidic-ferreous-mole; none otherwise. Session log: kilted-centauric-cardinal.","timestamp":1786901192751581001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Follow-on organic CI evidence while landing this fix: Gerrit 1827 (tests: isolate nested pytest temp cleanup) has LLM-Review +1, zero unresolved comments, but Verified -1 from run 31961655783 / job 95200612653 on Ubuntu 24.04, Python 3.11. The sole failure occurred after 13,517 passes: tests/unit/test_authorship_batched_ticket_map_7084.py::test_batched_map_matches_the_per_event_resolver_for_every_event. The ticket-scoped batched resolver returned an empty map, while the immediately following per-event resolver returned e40a20fd6aa32b4a9ceb3b237b174da80aa3dc20 for position 1000-aaaa1111. Python 3.12, Python 3.13, and macOS lanes passed. This is not being retried. Current source audit shows build_ticket_position_commit_map can yield {} through four observable classes: empty input, tracker/path resolution exception, subprocess exception/nonzero exit, or a zero/unparseable stdout stream. The CI log did not expose which class because the batched helper swallows diagnostics. No claim is yet made that this is the prior freshly-missing-object mechanism; the next step is a runtime trace / fault-injected reproduction that distinguishes those classes and tests whether another unisolated nested pytest cleanup can disturb this active tmp_path repository.","timestamp":1786904688796745001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Final landing evidence: after the unrelated authorship fixture-contract defect was root-caused and fixed in merged Gerrit 1828, change 1827 was revalidated without modifying patchset 1. Fresh run 31969784549 completed successfully with 17 successful jobs, 2 intentionally skipped route jobs, 0 failures, and 0 cancellations; the exact Ubuntu/Python 3.11 lane that previously failed passed. Gerrit then showed LLM-Review +1, Verified +1, submittable=true, and unresolved_comment_count=0. Plain Submit merged 1827 at 2026-08-16 20:34:56 UTC as 0f09b9add241f4a945af9af21557db23d34d2467. No old job was retried and no gate was bypassed.","timestamp":1786912515916150001}],"created_at":1786877315033783001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"90a02475-b810-4c33-b2fc-616e1cb8107a","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"760f-608f-4c69-449a"},{"link_uuid":"0daaf3fa-2dd6-4105-8c07-90efa8976b15","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"0bd6-4729-e79e-433b"},{"link_uuid":"feaef3cc-554c-4a8d-81d6-5fc14eb67377","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"24c3-35a6-fa43-4dc4"},{"link_uuid":"ab78d5d4-c1c7-49e9-90b0-f053b7ad2889","relation":"caused_by","target_id":"d540-fdd1-318f-49a5"}],"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe extras-enforced default suite intermittently timed out in tests/unit/test_test_suite_store_isolation.py after the captured nested pytest child had already printed 100%. The child was launched by tests/_store_isolation.py with a 90-second timeout but without a private --basetemp, so pytest session shutdown joined the user-global numbered temp root and synchronously scanned or deleted unrelated retained trees. This could consume the entire child timeout after the selected test itself succeeded.\n\n## Proven Mechanism and Repair\nA controlled real-filesystem A/B poisoned the shared numbered root with 400,000 stale entries: the shared-root child took 10.800s while the private-basetemp child took 0.882s and left the poison untouched. SIGUSR1 at the post-100% boundary captured the main thread in pytest cleanup_numbered_dir through recursive rmtree. The repair gives the nested child a caller-owned tmp_path/nested-pytest basetemp without changing selection, environment, capture, timeout, or ambient-store assertions.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] The liveness owner is proven: a live stack capture names pytest session-finish numbered-root cleanup, and controlled shared/private basetemp A/B shows cleanup cost scales with retained entries.\n- [x] The nested pytest child uses a caller-owned private basetemp and preserves the ambient tracker HEAD/directories plus a sentinel in another run's temp tree.\n- [x] Held-out real-subprocess coverage was RED before the repair because the nested child deleted another run's sentinel, GREEN after the repair, and RED again under the exact omitted-basetemp mutation before restoration.\n- [x] Repeated extras-enforced stress completed 25/25 real nested-pytest runs without timeout; the poisoned shared root remained unchanged.\n- [x] The in-scope focused tests, lint, typecheck, and full local suite are green (13,584 passed, 17 skipped, 288 deselected); no production code, dependency, timeout, or assertion was weakened.\n- [x] The independent omitted-basetemp sibling is tracked separately as acidic-ferreous-mole with discovered_from provenance.\n- [x] [operator-attested] Gerrit 1827 earned LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1 with zero unresolved comments after fresh run 31969784549 completed with 17 successful jobs, 2 route skips, and 0 failures; the exact Ubuntu/Python 3.11 lane passed, and plain Submit merged the change as 0f09b9add241f4a945af9af21557db23d34d2467.\n  provenance: environment=local worktree and Gerrit CI; principal=RebarBotNava, rebar review bot, and rebar CI bot; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — commands, process stacks, filesystem probes, votes, CI run, and merge state were observed directly and recorded in ticket comments.","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[{"path":"tests/_store_isolation.py","reason":"give real nested pytest isolation checks a caller-owned private basetemp"},{"path":"tests/unit/test_test_suite_store_isolation.py","reason":"held-out regression proving shared numbere